# Bit timing for the CTU CAN FD PCIe cards from this repo (LiteFury and
# artix_m2_canfd): 1 Mbit/s nominal sampled at 75 %, 8 Mbit/s data sampled at
# 70 %, the widest resynchronisation jump each allows.
#
# The mainline ctucanfd_pci driver assumes a 100 MHz core clock, but these
# designs clock the core at 80 MHz, so BitRate=/DataBitRate= would come out
# 20 % slow on the wire. The timing is therefore given in quanta (the driver
# turns 10 ns into prescaler 1, which is really 12.5 ns), exactly as
# ../can-up.sh does:
#   nominal 1 + 29 + 30 + 20 = 80 tq = 1 Mbit/s, data 1 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 10 tq = 8 Mbit/s
#
# The data phase is sampled early and resynchronises by up to 3 tq on purpose:
# nodes whose clock does not divide to 8 Mbit/s run a little off (an STM32G4
# at 170 MHz sends 8.095 Mbit/s, bits of 123.5 ns against our 125 ns), and
# with up to ten bits between resynchronising edges a sample point at 80 %
# drifts to 93 % of such a bit, which fails. The obot firmware's own 80 MHz
# nodes use the same 75 % / 70 % split.
#
# The name sorts before any generic "Type=can" file so that this one wins for
# these cards whatever their interfaces are called.
[Match]
Driver=ctucanfd_pci

[CAN]
TimeQuantaNSec=10
PropagationSegment=29
PhaseBufferSegment1=30
PhaseBufferSegment2=20
SyncJumpWidth=20
DataTimeQuantaNSec=10
DataPropagationSegment=3
DataPhaseBufferSegment1=3
DataPhaseBufferSegment2=3
DataSyncJumpWidth=3
FDMode=yes
RestartSec=100ms
